>Here is an excerpt from C.S. Lewis's _Voyage of the Dawn Treader_,
>where the character Eustace finds himself turned into a dragon and
>wants to write his story in the sand:
[...]


Why couldn't the dragon just make really big letters?

The problem with literary fictional justifications like this is that
it's hard to prove a negative...with a little fortitude someone can
probably dig up a passage from some other piece of fiction where the
author envisions dragons as having great manual dexterity.  I've read
enough books with dragons in them to believe this is probably the
case.  And I don't think Lewis is a quoted source on dragons in
nethack's help file.

I don't think there's a universal literary consensus on dragon writing
ability, in the same way there is, for example, on vampire biting
ability.  In the absence of that, I think it's best to be consistent
on the physics of things.

